-- the vestibule of EVERYONE IS HERE: the disagreements between the product as its team describes it
-- and the treaty as it stands, each a statement in the assay's own terms and the darkness it waits on.
-- a held statement seats when what it waits on arrives: the customer's word, a carrier, a reach of the
-- drawer's fragment, or a seating in the treaty's translator. read by bin/counter book.
-- the first round (2026-09-04, Abe's answers through isaac) seated six statements into the assay
-- (the_audience_hears_the_receipt_by_name, outside_the_audience_hears_no_receipt, the_sender_is_never_asked,
-- the_same_page_counts_only_within_earshot, the_payer_is_the_owner_or_the_host, edit_is_within_sight and
-- its two instances), refuted one (a member is a Membership; guests are never members; who can be asked is
-- the audience of the channel minus the sender), and left these.


-- Abe, in the room (2026-09-04): the day-of sheet is everyone's to edit with a memory, anyone opens a task and
-- only its owner, the couple, or the planner closes it — "yep that's great" — seated as
-- anyone_may_open_a_task and the_day_of_sheet_is_everyones_to_edit; the day lane's exclusion from tasks
-- still waits on lanes as a carrier, below.

-- the sixth round (the house's turn): Same page is a drawn sum — stated as a sum (List.sum over the
-- requests the viewer hears, mapped through tally) and read by the drawer as an aggregate, its two laws
-- re-proved over the filter; the season's total the same way, a face applied inside a lambda read as a
-- call to the face's function. a structural recursion (undo) and a pair-valued reading (venueTable) stay
-- beyond, and a list of lists (seasonView) is held as undrawn: the shadow holds no jagged array.

-- the seventh round (the house's turn): a lane is a member's sub-seat over the pages — memberSees subtracts
-- and never adds (lanes_shrink_never_lock, restated at the member), the day lane sees no task list and keeps
-- the day-of sheet, an edit is within the lane; the two page-number lists the assay carried since its first
-- day retired for the real thing.
-- the vestibule is empty (2026-09-04, isaac's move): the business, parked by Abe until a studio arrives,
-- cleared — if it is needed it comes back by being asked for; the repo does not remember on his behalf.
-- a held statement here is a disagreement between the customer's word and the treaty; today there is none.

-- the eighth round (2026-09-08, Abe between turns at league night, through isaac): the first one-liner
-- of the feature file, "a Role is one of: couple, planner, vendor, venue, party", refused at the first
-- word — "planner is vendor, or planner is couple." held: a planner is not a Role; a planner is a vendor
-- or the couple. waits on a carrier (the Role enum, and Member.kind, which may be where it already
-- lives) and on Abe's word for which one, when. the feature (assays/sheet.feature) follows him now.
-- the same round, his second word: "venue is a vendor. couple / vendor / helper (mother in law, wedding
-- party member, etc)." held: the venue is not a Role, it is a vendor; and the Role called party is a
-- helper, wider than the wedding party — a mother-in-law is one. so the Roles are three: couple, vendor,
-- helper. waits on the same carrier (the Role enum) and on the rules that hand each Role its pages, which
-- the treaty states for five and he states for three. the feature follows him.
-- his third word: "is a vendor a helper who gets paid? — yep." held: a vendor is not a Role; a vendor is
-- a helper with a truth, paid. so the Roles are two, couple and helper, and the pages a paid helper is
-- handed (the invoices, among them) are a rule over the role and the truth together. waits on a carrier
-- (paid, a truth on Member) and on a rule shape the feature grammar does not yet have — a sentence like
-- "a vendor is a helper who is paid" compiling to the retirement of a constructor and the minting of a
-- field. the feature keeps vendor as its own constructor until the grammar can say what he said.
-- his fourth word, on the pages: "yes. look at the demo. might be more but those are some." held: the
-- ten pages are a floor, not the list; the list waits on the demo. an enum is closed by construction, so
-- a page the demo has and the treaty lacks is a carrier waiting to be minted, and every rule over pages
-- widens when it lands. the sheet keeps the ten and says so.
-- his sixth word: "and Vendor Pro — they don't see any pages that have to do with just the vendor. My
-- season is only for season pass vendors." held: My season is a page (the demo's twelfth), seen by no
-- couple and by no unpaid helper, and by a paid helper only with the season pass — a SECOND truth on a
-- helper, pro, beside paid. the treaty has the season as a Thing the vendor owns (owner .season = .vendor,
-- seasonView, the_seasons_sum_is_over_my_own_invoices) and no truth for the pass; the sheet's grammar
-- has one truth per model tonight. waits on a carrier (pro) and on the grammar's sentence for a second
-- truth. the sheet keeps the page, keeps the couple and the unpaid out of it, and says nothing yet about
-- the paid helper there.
-- his seventh word came through his Claude, reading the demo: the mother of the bride is the wedding
-- party role, and what she sees depends on her LANE — the planning lane (home, the channels she is in,
-- tasks, same page, day-of, planning team, the seating chart with the guest names, editable, files, the
-- guide, her profile) or the helping-with-the-day lane (home, chats, same page, day-of, the chart, her
-- profile: tasks, team, files, and the guide drop away; the view shrinks, it doesn't lock). never: guests,
-- site, invoices and budget, the vendor room, my season, the who-can-edit panel. the treaty has the lane
-- (Member.dayLane, the_day_lane_sees_no_task_list, lanes_shrink_never_lock) and not files, guide, or team
-- dropping in the day lane; the sheet has one truth per model and cannot say the lane yet. held: the lane
-- as a third truth beside paid and pro, and five more pages on the floor — home, channels, files, the
-- guide, my profile — and the seating chart as floor plan and guest names in one page. and a fact for the
-- log: their walls table said invoices was off for the wedding party while the demo sidebar showed it; the
-- table won and the demo was fixed the same hour.
-- his eighth word: "what room?" — and "no bachelor party stuff. there's already an app for that." the
-- room is his word (one room for the whole wedding) and the sentence was the house's: a room is not
-- asked for anything, it has things. the bachelor party is a carrier the treaty holds — bach, withBach,
-- the_bach_wall, the_bach_parts_the_best_man, the best man's seat hearing it — that he refuses whole.
-- held: the treaty cuts a carrier the day this seats, and the best man's seat keeps the timeline. the
-- sheet has already let it go.
-- his ninth word, frame five: "the room has: the guests, the sheet, the timeline — it also has the seating
-- chart, the tasks, planning team, files, invoices." held: the treaty keeps an Ask (what the room holds as
-- data: guests, sheet, ledger, timeline, confirmed, vendorRoom) and a Page (the sidebar) as two lists, and
-- in his mouth they are one list — the room has the pages. and isaac's reading at the table: roles are a
-- payor (sponsor) and help, paid or not; a page's scope is who is handed it; the scopes he sees are
-- payor-only, payor-and-all-help, paid-help-only. a scope is a column's pattern in the grid, so the
-- drawer prints the scopes as a census rather than the treaty minting a type. waits on the treaty joining
-- Ask and Page, and on Abe's word for "sponsor" against "couple."
-- his tenth word, frames nine and ten: "the couple slot always needs to pay to have access to EIH, paid for
-- by themselves or the vendor" — and isaac's reading, "a room needs to be paid to be opened, doesn't matter
-- by who, just by someone": yup. a CONFIRMATION, not a hold: the treaty's lawful (the payer is the owner,
-- or a vendor paying for their couple), its rows ownWedding, coveredWedding, strayBill, and
-- the_payer_is_the_owner_or_the_host, seated on the fourth. and vendors pay for a season pass, which is
-- the season as a Thing the vendor owns. named at the table as a LAW OF ROOMS across three products
-- (softer's payment tier, yours' second day, EIH's couple slot): a room opens on a payment; the payment is
-- for the room and not for the occupant; the payer need not be the one who sits. a species candidate —
-- it knows no product's name — for Room's germ when the turn gets there.
-- his eleventh word: "the caterer sees: the timeline, the sheet" — "what sheet." the sheet is Roster's word
-- (the per-head meal sheet the room delivers, sheet gl) and the assay's probe; his sidebar says HEADCOUNT
-- and his day-of page is the day-of sheet. held: the probe's name waits on his word; the feature file that
-- was named for the wrong word twice is assays/everyone.feature now, his product's first word.
-- his thirteenth and fourteenth words: the best man, an unpaid helper who gets the timeline on top —
-- yep. the couple gets the guests, the headcount, and the timeline from the room — "yep and other
-- things." the treaty's coupleSeat hears the ledger and the confirmations too; the sheet's room holds
-- only guests, headcount, and timeline as data. held: the room's other things, in his words, when he
-- names them; the treaty's guess is the invoices and the confirmations.
-- his fifteenth word, a screenshot of the couple's sidebar, answering "what else does the room hold for
-- the couple": CHANNELS — #Everyone, and a private chat per vendor (Photography; Bloom & Vine Florals) the
-- couple is in, beside the vendor room they are not — and the BADGES: Guests 1 waiting (unanswered), Same
-- page 2/6 (samePage, the tally), Tasks 4, Invoices 2, Files 4 (lengths), Wedding site DRAFT (a state on
-- a page, which nothing in the treaty holds). the treaty has the channels (Channel.audience, visible,
-- inChannel, vendorRoomAudience, a_file_is_never_wider_than_its_channel) and the readings; the sheet's
-- grammar has neither channels nor a page's state. held: a sentence for a channel and its audience, a
-- sentence for a badge as a reading, and the draft as a truth on a page.
-- his sixteenth and seventeenth words. a private chat: "so far it's couples and one vendor. but I will
-- make group chats available." held: a private chat's audience is the couple and one vendor today — the
-- treaty's Channel.audience holds it as a list, which already affords the group chat he intends, so the
-- carrier waits on nothing and the rule (who may be added) waits on his word. the wedding site's DRAFT:
-- "idk?" — held with the darkness typed in his own vocabulary: UNKNOWN, the maintainer's own. an answer
-- of "I don't know" is an answer; the ask is covered, and the row waits on the demo (which may have
-- invented the badge) or on a day he decides. the first row of the night that waits on nobody's word
-- but the future's.
